1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,605
"Is this agent safe" invites a vague answer, because safety isn't one thing.

2
00:00:04,605 --> 00:00:09,140
"What's its blast radius" doesn't, because blast radius is exactly two questions:

3
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:12,891
can this action be undone, and how much does it cost if it's wrong.

4
00:00:12,891 --> 00:00:17,425
Answer both, honestly, and you know precisely where a guardrail actually needs to

5
00:00:17,425 --> 00:00:17,593
go.

6
00:00:17,593 --> 00:00:22,184
Ask "is it safe" in a review and you'll get a confident yes from whoever built it,

7
00:00:22,184 --> 00:00:26,607
because they're answering about the agent's intelligence, not its consequences.

8
00:00:26,607 --> 00:00:30,974
Ask about blast radius instead and the conversation moves to specific actions,

9
00:00:30,974 --> 00:00:34,501
specific numbers, and a specific answer you can actually check.

10
00:00:34,501 --> 00:00:35,620
This isn't abstract.

11
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:39,763
It's the direct continuation of 6.2's read-versus-write question and 6.4's

12
00:00:39,763 --> 00:00:44,298
escalate-to-a-human step, given the actual framework that tells you which actions

13
00:00:44,298 --> 00:00:45,474
need which treatment.

14
00:00:45,474 --> 00:00:50,176
Every lecture in this section so far has been building toward this one without quite

15
00:00:50,176 --> 00:00:50,736
naming it.

16
00:00:50,736 --> 00:00:53,368
Tools and MCP gave you what an agent can reach.

17
00:00:53,368 --> 00:00:56,447
The reliability maths gave you how often it goes wrong.

18
00:00:56,447 --> 00:01:00,870
Guardrails are where those two facts turn into an actual decision about where a

19
00:01:00,870 --> 00:01:04,061
human has to stand between the agent and the consequence.

20
00:01:04,611 --> 00:01:09,459
Read this table by the two questions, not by the dollar figure alone, because dollar

21
00:01:09,459 --> 00:01:12,345
amount on its own misleads you in both directions.

22
00:01:12,345 --> 00:01:16,905
A twenty-pound refund that's trivially clawed back if it's wrong doesn't need a

23
00:01:16,905 --> 00:01:21,754
human standing between the agent and the action, whatever your instinct about "money

24
00:01:21,754 --> 00:01:22,505
moving" says.

25
00:01:22,505 --> 00:01:27,295
An email to a journalist costs nothing and is completely irreversible the moment it

26
00:01:27,295 --> 00:01:27,642
sends.

27
00:01:27,642 --> 00:01:32,490
That belongs in the bottom row with the eight-hundred-pound refund, not the top one,

28
00:01:32,490 --> 00:01:36,589
because reversibility, not price, is doing the real work in this table.

29
00:01:36,589 --> 00:01:41,206
Most teams default to gating by dollar amount because it's the easiest number to

30
00:01:41,206 --> 00:01:42,361
write into a policy.

31
00:01:42,361 --> 00:01:44,439
It's also the wrong axis on its own.

32
00:01:44,439 --> 00:01:48,883
A five-pound action that posts publicly and can't be taken back deserves more

33
00:01:48,883 --> 00:01:53,732
caution than a five-hundred-pound one your finance system can reverse with one call.

34
00:01:53,732 --> 00:01:58,465
Ask both questions every time, not just the one that's easiest to put a number on.

35
00:01:59,015 --> 00:02:03,506
The difference between these two is the difference between a policy and a feeling.

36
00:02:03,506 --> 00:02:05,916
"We review its work" describes an intention.

37
00:02:05,916 --> 00:02:10,406
A named gate on a named action is enforced in the system itself, the same boundary

38
00:02:10,406 --> 00:02:14,240
6.2 drew between what the model requests and what your system permits.

39
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:18,621
Build the gate so the agent cannot technically complete the gated action without

40
00:02:18,621 --> 00:02:22,564
approval, not so that a reviewer is supposed to catch it after the fact.

41
00:02:22,564 --> 00:02:26,781
A policy a tired reviewer can skip under deadline pressure isn't a guardrail.

42
00:02:26,781 --> 00:02:29,190
It's a suggestion with a job title attached.

43
00:02:29,190 --> 00:02:33,407
Test whether a gate is real with one question: can the action complete if the

44
00:02:33,407 --> 00:02:36,146
approver is on holiday and nobody covers for them.

45
00:02:36,146 --> 00:02:40,527
If the answer is yes, eventually, through a timeout or a default-approve setting

46
00:02:40,527 --> 00:02:43,813
someone added for convenience, it was never actually a gate.

47
00:02:43,813 --> 00:02:45,729
It was a delay with an expiry date.

48
00:02:46,279 --> 00:02:50,988
Say the honest caveat, because the instinct to add a gate to everything after seeing

49
00:02:50,988 --> 00:02:55,641
that table is understandable and it defeats the entire point of automating the task

50
00:02:55,641 --> 00:02:56,707
in the first place.

51
00:02:56,707 --> 00:03:00,350
What's the point of an agent whose every action waits on a human?

52
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:01,079
None, really.

53
00:03:01,079 --> 00:03:05,732
It isn't faster than doing the task yourself, and it quietly trains the approver to

54
00:03:05,732 --> 00:03:09,432
click approve without reading, which is worse than no gate at all.

55
00:03:09,432 --> 00:03:13,973
Reserve gates for where this lecture's table actually puts them, the irreversible

56
00:03:13,973 --> 00:03:17,000
and the expensive, and let the top row run on its own.

57
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:18,906
That's not a compromise on safety.

58
00:03:18,906 --> 00:03:23,335
It's what makes the safety on the rows that matter credible instead of theatre.

59
00:03:23,335 --> 00:03:26,138
Gate fatigue is the specific failure worth naming.

60
00:03:26,138 --> 00:03:30,735
An approver who sees forty low-stakes approval requests a day stops reading any of

61
00:03:30,735 --> 00:03:35,444
them carefully by request twelve, and the one genuinely dangerous request that slips

62
00:03:35,444 --> 00:03:39,537
through on day thirty gets the exact same reflexive click as the harmless

63
00:03:39,537 --> 00:03:40,770
thirty-nine before it.

64
00:03:40,770 --> 00:03:45,199
Fewer, better-chosen gates protect people better than many indiscriminate ones,

65
00:03:45,199 --> 00:03:49,627
precisely because attention is the scarce resource a gate is actually spending.

66
00:03:50,177 --> 00:03:54,758
That's the definition worth keeping, and the second half matters as much as the

67
00:03:54,758 --> 00:03:59,629
first: blast radius has nothing to do with how impressive the agent's plan looked in

68
00:03:59,629 --> 00:04:00,035
review.

69
00:04:00,035 --> 00:04:04,500
A beautifully reasoned plan that ends in an irreversible, costly action still

70
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:06,008
belongs in the bottom row.

71
00:04:06,008 --> 00:04:07,805
Run the sort on something real.

72
00:04:07,805 --> 00:04:12,502
Most agents, once every action is actually listed instead of described in general

73
00:04:12,502 --> 00:04:16,967
terms, turn out to have one or two genuinely high-stakes actions hiding among

74
00:04:16,967 --> 00:04:18,243
several harmless ones.

75
00:04:18,243 --> 00:04:23,114
Finding exactly which ones they are is the whole value of doing this exercise before

76
00:04:23,114 --> 00:04:25,201
launch instead of after an incident.

77
00:04:25,201 --> 00:04:28,680
Do the sort before the architecture is finalised, not after.

78
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:33,203
It's far cheaper to redesign an agent so its one irreversible action becomes a

79
00:04:33,203 --> 00:04:38,074
proposal a human confirms, than to bolt a gate onto a system that was built assuming

80
00:04:38,074 --> 00:04:42,133
full autonomy everywhere and now has to be pulled apart to fit one in.

81
00:04:42,683 --> 00:04:44,295
One thing before you move on.

82
00:04:44,295 --> 00:04:48,796
Do the sort completely, not just for the action that worried you most walking in.

83
00:04:48,796 --> 00:04:52,685
The ones you'd forgotten to mention are usually where the real gap is.

84
00:04:52,685 --> 00:04:56,853
Then name an actual person for every gated action, not a team or a process.

85
00:04:56,853 --> 00:04:58,964
"The support team" is not an approver.

86
00:04:58,964 --> 00:05:01,687
A specific name who knows they own that click is.

87
00:05:01,687 --> 00:05:06,299
Next lecture asks the question this whole section has been building toward from the

88
00:05:06,299 --> 00:05:10,800
other direction: when the honest answer isn't which guardrail to add, but whether

89
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:12,523
this should be an agent at all.
